---
import Profile from '../layouts/Profile.astro';
import { profile as p } from '../lib/profile';
---
<Profile lang="en">
<section class="section" id="about">
  <h2>About</h2>
  <p class="lead">
    Hi, I'm Xiao! I'm a Ph.D. student in
    <a href={p.department_link} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Electrical and Computer Engineering</a>
    at Iowa State University, working with
    <a href={p.advisor_link} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{p.advisor}</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    I'm fascinated by a question that sounds almost too simple: <em>why does plain gradient descent work so
    well on certain problems that look hopelessly nonconvex on paper?</em> To find out, I map optimization
    landscapes. I look for the valleys, ridges, and saddle points of structured low-rank problems, such as
    recovering a signal and its blur kernel from magnitude-only measurements, or sensing tensors through
    their Fourier slices. Once I understand the geometry, I like to turn those insights into faster
    algorithms. More recently, I've been exploring how Muon-style orthogonalized updates can accelerate
    tensor regression.
  </p>
  <p>
    My path here has a few turns in it. I studied data science in Beijing at North China University of
    Technology (2018&ndash;2022), then computer science at Washington University in St. Louis (2022&ndash;2024),
    and spent some time as an intern at <a href="https://www.huawei.com/en/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><strong>Huawei</strong></a> before starting my Ph.D. in Ames. Iowa is
    famously flat, so these days the interesting terrain lives in my loss functions.
  </p>

  <ul class="interests" aria-label="Research interests">
    {p.interests.map((i) => <>{'\n'}<li set:html={i} /></>)}
  </ul>

  <figure class="playground" aria-label="Interactive optimization landscape">
    <canvas id="landscape" width="880" height="260" role="img"
      aria-label="Contour plot of a function with two global minima and a saddle point between them"></canvas>
    <figcaption>
      <span class="pg-title"><i class="fa-solid fa-hand-pointer" aria-hidden="true"></i> Try it</span>
      Click anywhere to drop a ball and watch gradient descent roll downhill. The landscape has a saddle in
      the middle, but no spurious minima, so every ball ends at one of the two global solutions, &plusmn;x&#9733;.
      That sign ambiguity is exactly what phase retrieval has to live with.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>
</Profile>
